joining us now is former chief constable  of greater manchester and that is safita  farhi um hello to sir peter thanks for  joining us on the program this morning  um  tough time for the communities of  liverpool how do you start to keep a  city together when something like this  happens  well in the aftermath of an incredible  uh awful tragedy like this people do  come together um and you know serena  kennedy the chief constable has made it  clear  that she needs the support of the whole  community but it's not just about  arresting this particular individual who  committed this crime it is about the  criminal network behind them and it's  whoever paid for this it's whoever  organized it whoever transported the  gunman to the scene um you know is about  a whole criminal uh outfit there so it's  not just about this individual  horrendous crime it is about the fact  that often communities in some of our  cities have to live with this sort of  organized criminality around them and  subverting the normal system uh  operating things like the drugs trade  the market in stolen goods illegal money  lending whatever it might be um you know  and it needs all the agencies and local  people working together long term uh to  undermine this  speaking about the chief constable she  has asked criminals in the area to  examine their consciences and cooperate  with the police from your experience do  they  it's very rare really you know this at  the end of the day will be down to  either a lucky piece of information uh  from the public uh really good analysis  of of things like mobile data mobile  phone data  forensics uh sometimes another criminal  gang will see it to their advantage  in a way  to pass the you know forward information  which might lead to the uh to the  disruption of another criminal gang  so you know this mate may be  cleared very quickly it may take many  many months a very detailed  investigation but as i say it is about  the long-term aim the long-term effort  to undermine these criminal gangs  as i say which cause misery to lots of  communities we often if we those of us  who live in nicer areas don't feel it  but the fact is uh the market in stolen  goods when cars are stolen uh when  things like watches are stolen in some  of the awful muggings we're saying  seeing in places in london there are  criminal gangs behind this and what we  saw with this awful murder um is the  fact just the sheer villain you know  that the callousness and the viciousness  of some of these gangs who are prepared  to use horrendous level of violence  that's how they maintain their power and  their intimidation  sometimes innocent members of the public  get caught up in that but often when  criminal  members suffer the most horrendous  injuries they refuse to cooperate the  police or tell the police what's  happened because either they want to  exert their own revenge or just out of  fear  yeah what about this character who's in  hospital at the moment his mates came  picked him up left basically walked over  a dying child in order to pick him up  and take him to hospital the police will  want to talk to him as a mattress  emergency  absolutely and we'll want to talk to the  people around him and absolutely  investigate the whole background  exhaustively about this they may well  the police may well have a very good  idea about you know who's behind this um  what is the particular feud what's the  particular dispute that this has been  played out within  but the fact is you know they have to  get it to the criminal standard uh you  know to achieve a conviction and for any  arrests to be you know to ultimately  lead to a person being behind bars um  you know so absolutely they're going to  be investigating every different aspect  of that they're going to be relying on  cctv in the area but ultimately as i say  it's about long-term intelligence  gathering uh working with people like  the national crime agency and the  regional organized crime unit and other  forces uh but crucially other agencies  in the city of liverpool to share  information to share powers as i say not  only to arrest this individual but to  you know take apart the whole criminal  network behind them  olivia's mom will never  ever get over what happened in the  hallway of her home olivia's siblings  will never get over it but how do the  the police officers in liverpool the  police service in liverpool start to  make other  um  neighbors feel safe in their own homes  well it's through you know really good  local neighborhood policing it's been  there visible in the local community  it's about the local people having  confidence and the ability of their  police force to take this apart and  sadly merseyside police great the  manchester police says middleton's  police um are used to this ongoing  criminal activity and working with the  local people and trying to reassure them  uh but ultimately you know people will  only have confidence if they see these  criminal outfits being taken apart and  their power undermined and local people  have a role to play in this but it often  can be very very difficult and people  are going through really difficult times  and then approached by people like  illegal money lenders who say that  they're going to help them  but actually just horrendously exploit  them uh young people are drawn into the  criminal outfits into things like the  county lines networks and exploited and  young people who possibly could do well  at school um are attracted you know  thinking that they could be the next mr  big they could be you know the next  person driving around in a big bmw and  so these criminal outfits they create an  alternative value system and sometimes  almost uh trying to achieve an  alternative criminal justice system you  know and that's how deep-seated some of  this is um and why you know i know all  the agencies are working together and  sadly you know lots of police forces are  extremely experienced in dealing with  this and you know they'll be making  every effort now today to not only get  these people arrested but at the same  time try to reassure local people and  making sure local people have confidence  you know in the system in the criminal  justice system you know in in in the  core agencies and don't feel that they  have to rely on criminal outfits um you  know to help them get through life  okay so peter thank you

